# Testing — TDD and Conventions

This rule applies to any test file. Tests are part of the code, held to the same quality bar as production code.

## When to TDD

- **Domain logic and application interactors:** prefer test-first. Write a failing unit test that captures the behavior, make it pass with the smallest change, then refactor.
- **Adapters and presentation:** test-after is acceptable, but every adapter must have at least one integration test.
- **Bug fixes:** always start with a failing test that reproduces the bug. The test, not the fix, is the proof.

## Test Pyramid

```
       /\
      /e2\        few, slow, real environment
     /----\
    / int  \      adapter-level, real I/O via Testcontainers / pytest fixtures
   /--------\
  /   unit   \    many, fast, deterministic, no IO
 /------------\
```

- Default to **unit tests** for domain + application.
- Add **integration tests** when behavior spans layers (e.g. a repository roundtrip, an outbox relay producing a real RabbitMQ message).
- Reserve **e2e** for golden-path user flows.

## Structure

- Tests **mirror `src/`**.
  - Java: `src/test/java/<package>/<MirroredPath>Test.java`.
  - Python: `tests/{unit,integration,e2e}/...` with the same package layout as `src/`.

## Test Body

- Use **Arrange / Act / Assert**:
  ```python
  async def test_create_user_persists_new_user_when_email_is_unique() -> None:
      # Arrange
      command = CreateUserCommand(email="x@y.com", name="x", password="pw")

      # Act
      view = await handler(command)

      # Assert
      assert view.user_id is not None
  ```
- Comments inside test bodies are limited to the `# Arrange / # Act / # Assert` markers and parametrization case descriptions. No other comments.
- One behavior per test. If you find yourself writing `and` in the test name, split it.

## Naming

- Behavior + expected outcome.
  - Good: `shouldRejectWhenEmailAlreadyExists`, `test_publish_emits_lesson_published_event`.
  - Bad: `testCreate`, `test_user_1`, `it_works`.
- Avoid "happy path" / "edge case" labels. The test name is the spec.

## Isolation

- Tests are deterministic. No network, no real time, no real filesystem in unit tests. Stub clocks (`Clock` port) and ID generators.
- Tests are independent: any test can run alone, in any order, in parallel.
- Reset shared state between tests. No `static` mutable state in Java tests, no module-level singletons in Python tests.

## Test Doubles

- **Fakes** (in-memory repository implementing the port) — preferred for unit-testing an interactor.
- **Stubs** (canned answers) — fine for adapters' boundaries.
- **Mocks** (verify interactions) — use sparingly. Verifying behavior is fine; verifying that a method was called three times is fragile.
- Never mock the type you own. Mock at the seam (port / interface), not on a concrete class.

## Coverage

- Coverage is a signal, not a goal. Aim for `~80%+` on `domain/` and `application/`. Do not chase 100% by testing trivial getters.
- A green build with weak assertions is worse than a few tests with strong assertions.

## Validation

| Service           | Test command          |
|-------------------|-----------------------|
| Java services     | `./gradlew test`      |
| `answer_service`  | `pytest -v`           |
| `frontend`        | (no test framework configured) |

Tests must be green before reporting work done. A flaky test is a bug — quarantine and file an issue, do not retry until it passes.
